  <content class="introduction">
    <figure id="fig-00001" class="splash">
    <media alt="Photo of two adults with two adolescents outside.">
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_08_00_Identity.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder. (credit: modification of work “Family photo” by “donita"/nappy, Public Domain)</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00001">Aliyah and her twin brother Omar recently moved to North America from Lebanon, and it has been an exciting yet overwhelming experience for them. Although Aliyah still considers herself Lebanese, she now is expected to swear allegiance to the U.S. flag in her daily fifth-grade class activities. Omar is discovering that gender expectations are different in the United States; in Lebanon, he was brought up to be more distant and respectful toward girls outside his family, but now he is expected to be friendly and forward.</para>
    <para id="para-00002">Aliyah has pushed herself into her schoolwork and is trying to catch up. While she speaks English and Arabic, she is struggling to read assignments in English, and this has made her quieter than usual at school. She used to have many friends, but now she has a harder time connecting with her peers. Omar is less worried about school and spends most of his time talking and joking, even if the teacher asks him to stop. He just finds school so exciting, with all the social activities and new experiences, and after school he is always playing with new friends at the park.</para>
    <para id="para-00003">Aliyah and Omar’s parents have recently met with the school principal to discuss how the twins are adjusting. They’ve been advised to be patient because Aliyah and Omar are adapting to a new culture in addition to a new school. The staff are encouraged that Omar seems to have found a group of other kids to play soccer with during recess. The school counselor believes that Aliyah will begin making friends once she feels more confident with the social and cultural etiquette and learns the regional slang words. For now, she recommends that both Aliyah and Omar join a local peer mentoring group to help with social and emotional skills while continuing to take their regular classes. The principal explained to Aliyah and Omar’s parents that because the schoolteachers and staff have training in cultural inclusivity and socioemotional development, the twins will have a great deal of support to improve their social skills and adjust quickly. Although their parents believe this to be true and have already seen remarkable progress, they wonder:</para>
    <list id="list-00001">
    <item>Will an increase in confidence make it easier to make friends?</item>
    <item>Will Omar and Aliyah lose their cultural identity as they become immersed in a new culture?</item>
    <item>Do teachers or peers influence social and emotional development much?</item>
    <item>Do peer mentoring groups and other school programs help?</item>
    </list>
    <para id="para-00004">In this chapter, you will learn about the significant social and emotional milestones and dilemmas faced as children in middle childhood, ages seven to twelve years, progress through development.</para>
    </content>
  </document>